  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "The short answer",
      "items": [
        "Two browser pages talk HTTPS to Cloudflare, which relays through an outbound tunnel to a single Bun process on the Ubuntu host",
        "Inside that process one router sends every request through the access-key gate (or the narrow guest-link door) to the upload engine, guest links or the zip streamer, which share SQLite for metadata and a folder of randomly-named files for bytes"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "Not shown here",
      "items": [
        "The Zip streamer and the file store (shown in the 'Download all' and upload questions), the expiry sweeper, and deployment (auto-deploy, admin scripts, env file), which have their own questions",
        "proposed features",
        "Connected but out of scope: Zip streamer, Expiry sweeper, Env file, File bytes on disk, Auto-deploy timer, Admin scripts"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Constraints",
      "items": [
        "Run exactly one server process: the in-flight upload set and the login rate limiter live in memory; two instances would each believe they own an upload",
        "Only code inside the Bun process reads or writes the database: the browsers and the edge reach data only through the HTTP routes, where the auth gate and guest scope are enforced"
      ]
    }
  ]
